Function 1: Duty / Advice / Obligation

Should = expresses what's the right/advisable thing to do

Examples:

We should obey the rules of the country. (general duty/advice)
He should have done the work. (should have + V3 = past obligation that wasn't fulfilled — a regret/criticism about the past)
It was very cold; he should have brought his coat. (same pattern — didn't happen, but should have)
Function 2: Condition (replacing "If" — Inversion)

Should + subject + V1 = an alternative, more formal way of saying "If + subject + V1"

Examples:

If he works, he will succeed.
= Should he work, he will succeed. (same meaning, formal/inverted structure)
If it rains, we will stay indoors.
= Should it rain, we will stay indoors.

Why this matters: In this "condition" use, notice the verb stays base form ("work," not "works") 
because "should" is a modal — same rule as Rule 24 (modal + base verb). This inverted structure is common in formal/written English 
and often tested in exams as an alternate way to express "if."

Had Better + Base Form (V1)

"Had better" is a modal-like expression that must always be followed by the BASE FORM (V1) of the verb — never the past tense, never "to + verb."

The Structure

Had better + V1 (base form)

Example

(a) ❌ You had better gone to school on time.
✅ You had better go to school on time.

"Had better" looks like past tense because of "had," but it's not — it's a fixed modal expression meaning "it would be wiser/advisable to...", 
always referring to present/future advice.
Just like other modals (can, should, must, will), it's followed by the plain base verb, not a past form.
